  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on county-level aggregated data, which may mask neighborhood-level variations in eviction dynamics and aid distribution.",
      "what": "Using a panel dataset spanning 2009 to 2018 across coastal U.S. counties, this study analyzed how hurricane frequency and federal disaster aid affect eviction filings and threats. The analysis reveals that hurricanes drive up eviction risks by inflating local rents during the disaster year and for up to two subsequent years. Conversely, higher levels of aggregated federal aid are associated with a reduction in eviction filings and threats four years after a disaster occurs.",
      "why": "The findings establish a direct, multi-year empirical link between natural disasters, rising rents, and renter displacement, while demonstrating that long-term federal aid eventually acts as a stabilizing force to reduce eviction risks."
    }
  },
